<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raqqada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/876">876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raqqada was a palace city near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, founded in 876 by the Aghlabid emir Ibrahim II as his residence. In 909 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama</a> army of the Fatimid movement took it, and in January 910 Abdallah al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> there. It remained a Fatimid seat until the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a>. Its site now houses a museum of Islamic art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raqqada.jpg" alt="Raqqada" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: IssamBarhoumi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bassins_Arabe_de_Raqada_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ibrahim II ibn Ahmad, the ninth Aghlabid emir, moved his residence from al-Abbasiyya to find a quiet place away from the noise of the city, and Raqqada became the dynasty's second capital, about ten kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>. It was furnished with several palaces and a mosque, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> set up a textile and paper factory there to supply the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a>, the Bayt al-Hikma. For a time the city grew even larger than Kairouan.</p><p>Abdullah al-Mahdi Billah, founder of the Fatimid dynasty, settled at Raqqada after first moving to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, but he later chose another capital and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a>. After the troops of the fourth Fatimid caliph, al-Muizz, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> on 7 July 969 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> was founded as the new capital, Raqqada was demolished. After 1960 a presidential palace was built on part of the site.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The palaces have been only partly excavated, but remains spread over about 9 square kilometres show that the city grew large. Carved stucco similar to that of Abbasid Samarra and glazed pottery with a metallic sheen have been found. The one palace studied, probably the Qasr as-Sahn or Courtyard Palace, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mudbrick</a> enclosure of 55 by 55 metres with round buttresses, a central courtyard over a cistern, a bent entrance and a columned reception hall with an apse that probably marked the emir's seat; it was later enlarged to 105 by 105 metres.</p><p>North-east of it lay a water basin between 90 and 130 metres wide, perhaps part of the Qasr al-Bahr or Water Palace named in the sources, the earliest such palace in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> region. Ibrahim II's own residence, the Qasr al-Fath or Palace of Victory, has not been located.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Since 1986 the site has housed the National Museum of Islamic Art. Its collections include ceramics from the 9th and 10th centuries, when Raqqada was occupied, finds from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> and al-Mansuriyya, coins spanning more than six centuries of the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> from the library of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan">Great Mosque of Kairouan</a>, among them leaves of the 10th-century Blue Koran.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raqqada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Residence shifted from al-Abbasiyya to <b>Raqqada</b>, the palace-city Ibrahim II founded in 876 with a mosque, baths, a market and several palaces, where he and his successors lived in the Qasr al-Fath except for periods spent in Tunis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 909 his Kutama army overthrew the Aghlabid emirate, and the imam was retrieved from Sijilmasa and proclaimed caliph at <b>Raqqada</b> in January 910 as Abdallah al-Mahdi Billah, establishing a rival caliphate to Baghdad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
